body:
Format migration: the second half of `kalide upgrade` — carrying a project's structural/format version forward across kalide versions. The anchors are a template library's required `library.yaml` `format:` integer (domain.value.library-manifest; today only `1` is accepted — internal/template.loadLibraryMeta rejects any other value) and the running binary's build-stamped version (domain.value.version, the `main.version` printed by version-command). A migration is a defined, ordered, versioned transformation from one source version/format to the next — for example a `library.yaml` `format:` bump with the schema rewrites it governs, or a `template.yaml` schema change (domain.entity.format-migration). Upgrade applies the defined migrations from the project's current version to the running kalide's version, in order, each at most once, preserving author values (a library's `name`/`description`, a deck's title/author/date/properties) and changing only the format/structure the migration governs.

A format upgrade does not recognize is reported, never guessed or silently migrated: a library whose `format:` is unknown or newer than the running kalide is left byte-for-byte untouched and the command exits non-zero naming the format found and the format supported (global.constraint.upgrade-unknown-format-reported). A deck, which has no version field, is never assumed to be at any version: a version-sensitive deck migration runs only when it can be proven from the deck's own content, otherwise upgrade reports that it cannot determine the deck's version and leaves the format-sensitive files unchanged (requirements.note.upgrade-deck-vs-library). Migrations are defined by kalide, not derived by guessing from the project's files, and the result of a migration is validated before it is kept (a library re-loads through the template library loader; a deck through the deck loader); a multi-file migration is all-or-nothing, so a failure leaves the files it would have written as they were (global.constraint.upgrade-migrations-ordered-atomic, global.constraint.upgrade-never-breaks-project).

AC:
- a library at a known older `format:` is migrated in order to the running kalide's format, its `name`/`description` preserved, and afterwards loads cleanly through the template library loader;
- a library whose `format:` is unknown or newer than the running kalide fails non-zero, names the format found and the format supported, and is left byte-for-byte untouched;
- a migration that cannot complete leaves every file it would have written byte-for-byte unchanged (no partial migration);
- a deck whose version cannot be established from its content is not assumed to be at any version; version-sensitive migrations are skipped and reported;
- the upgraded project validates: `kalide start` succeeds for a deck and the library loads cleanly (templates-dir-validation).
